          {
            "typeName": "dsDescription",
            "multiple": true,
            "typeClass": "compound",
            "value": [
              {
                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "Introduction. Europe has been dealing with an increasing number of refugees during\r\nthe past 5 years. The timing of screening and vaccination of refugees is debated by\r\nmany professionals, however refugees’ perspectives on health issues are infrequently\r\ntaken into account. In this study, we aimed to investigate asylum seekers’ perspectives\r\non infectious diseases screening and vaccination policies.\r\nMaterials and Methods. Interviews were conducted in Greece and the Netherlands.\r\nAsylum seekers and recently arrived refugees were approached and informed with the\r\nhelp of interpreters; consent forms were acquired. The survey focused on demographic\r\ndata, vaccination status, screening policies and prevention of infectious diseases.\r\nResults. A total of 61 (43 male, 70.5%) refugees (30 Afghanis, 16 Syrian, 7 Erithrean)\r\nwere interviewed. Mean age was 35.2 years (SD 13.5) and 50% had received primary\r\nor secondary education, while 24.6% received none. Median time after arrival in\r\nGreece and the Netherlands was 24 months (IQR 8.5-28). 44 out of 61 (72.1)\r\nparticipants were willing to be vaccinated after arrival in Europe, 26 preferred\r\nvaccination and screening to be performed at the point of entry. The need for\r\nscreening and vaccination was perceived higher amongst participants in Greece\r\n(100% vs 43.3%) due to living conditions leading to increased risk of outbreaks.\r\nConclusion. Participants were willing to communicate their perspectives and concerns.\r\nScreening and vaccination programs could be more effective when implemented\r\nshortly after arrival and by involving asylum seekers and refugees when developing\r\nscreening and vaccination interventions."
                },
                "dsDescriptionDate": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionDate",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "2019"
                }
              }
            ]
